\title{Backward-Error Semantics for Floating-Point Chaotic Programs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{1 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We propose and develop a two-layer theory of the numerical simulation of polynomial dynamical
systems in floating-point arithmetic, in which the \emph{semantics} of the arithmetic is
rigorously separated from the \emph{dynamics} of the map. The semantic layer establishes that a
finite floating-point execution of a polynomial iteration, in a run free of overflow, underflow
and exceptional values, is \textbf{exactly} the orbit of a nonautonomous polynomial system whose
coefficients lie in a relative $\gamma_{2n}(u)$-neighbourhood of the nominal ones, where $u$ is
the unit roundoff, $n$ the number of coefficients, and $\gamma_k(u)=(1+u)^k-1$. As a corollary
the execution is an exact $\delta$-pseudo-orbit of the exact real map with the compositional
defect $\delta=\gamma_{2n}(u)\sum_i|a_i|B^i$, $B$ being an observed magnitude bound. This layer
uses no dynamical hypothesis whatsoever. The dynamical layer is then a black box consuming the
defect certificate: forward tracking from a fixed initial condition yields the bound
$\delta(L^n-1)/(L-1)$, which we show is \emph{attained}, hence sharp; backward tracking along
inverse branches of an expanding map yields the bound $\delta/(\lambda-1)$, \emph{uniform in the
number of steps}. A nonautonomous refinement replaces the global Lipschitz constant by observed
local expansion factors, giving an a-posteriori certificate computable from the execution itself.
Instantiations give explicit numbers: a double-precision execution of the logistic map $4z(1-z)$
observed to remain in $[0,1]$ is shadowed by the exact real orbit through the same initial point
to within $2^{-46}(4^n-1)/3$; a double-precision execution of the expanding cubic $z^3+2z$
observed to remain within magnitude $B$ is shadowed by an exact orbit to within
$\gamma_8(u)(2B+B^3)$ at \emph{every} step. Finally we isolate a phenomenon we call
\emph{structural backward error}: the natural three-operation implementation of the logistic step
is exactly a logistic step at a detuned parameter, a statement about the \emph{program} rather
than the mathematical function, which fails for the algebraically equivalent expanded form.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} backward error analysis, floating-point arithmetic, unit roundoff,
Horner's rule, pseudo-orbit, shadowing, chaotic dynamics, logistic map, expanding maps,
a-posteriori error certificates.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}

It is a commonplace of computational science that chaotic systems cannot be reliably simulated.
The argument is a syllogism: chaotic systems amplify perturbations at an exponential rate
$e^{\lambda t}$; floating-point arithmetic perturbs every operation; hence after
$O(\lambda^{-1}\log(1/u))$ steps the computed trajectory bears no relation to the intended one.
The conclusion is correct as far as it goes, but the argument is informal in a specific and
repairable way: it treats rounding as an \emph{unmodelled disturbance} --- an appeal to
``precision'' --- and then hands that disturbance to a dynamical amplification argument.

This paper's thesis is that the appeal to precision should be replaced by a theorem, and that the
theorem is a \emph{semantic} one: it says which exact mathematical problem the program solved.
The classical technology for such statements is Wilkinson's backward error analysis. What is new
here is the observation that backward error analysis is exactly the right interface to the
classical shadowing theory of dynamical systems, and that the composition of the two produces
fully explicit, a-posteriori-checkable certificates for real programs. The two layers are:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
\item \textbf{(S) Semantics.} A finite floating-point execution, under a runtime-verifiable
hypothesis (no overflow, observed magnitudes bounded), is an exact real pseudo-orbit with a
computable local defect.
\item \textbf{(D) Dynamics.} A pseudo-orbit with a given local defect is tracked by a true orbit,
with an error governed by the expansion properties of the map.
\end{itemize}

Layer (S) contains all the arithmetic and no dynamics. Layer (D) contains all the dynamics and no
arithmetic. The folk theorem conflates them; separating them tells us exactly which half is
responsible for what, and allows each half to be improved independently.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em]
\item A backward-error semantics for rounded Horner evaluation (Theorem~\ref{thm:backward}).
\item A local defect certificate (Theorem~\ref{thm:defect}) and a proof that its first-order term
in $u$ cannot be removed (Theorem~\ref{thm:sharpdefect}).
\item A semantic translation theorem (Theorem~\ref{thm:translation}) and the exact nonautonomous
representation of an execution (Theorem~\ref{thm:nonauto}).
\item Sharpness of forward shadowing (Theorem~\ref{thm:sharpshadow}).
\item Uniform-in-time shadowing for expanding maps (Theorem~\ref{thm:uniform}) and its
instantiation to a concrete expanding cubic (Theorem~\ref{thm:cubic}).
\item A-posteriori nonautonomous shadowing (Theorem~\ref{thm:variable}) with an explicit
instantiation for double-precision logistic executions (Theorem~\ref{thm:apost}).
\item Structural backward error (Theorem~\ref{thm:structural}) and a boundary result showing that
the runtime hypothesis is load-bearing (Theorem~\ref{thm:boundary}).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Relation to classical material}

Backward error analysis for polynomial evaluation and the constants $\gamma_k$ are classical
numerical analysis. Shadowing of pseudo-orbits by true orbits is classical hyperbolic dynamics.
What is developed here is the \emph{interface}: the precise form of certificate that layer (S)
can produce and layer (D) can consume, the demonstration that the interface is lossless, and the
demonstration that each layer's bound is sharp in its own regime.

\section{The model of computation}

Throughout, $u\ge 0$ denotes the \emph{unit roundoff}: $u=2^{-24}$ for IEEE binary32 and
$u=2^{-53}$ for binary64.

\begin{definition}[Rounding model]\label{def:model}
A \emph{rounding model} with unit roundoff $u\ge0$ consists of three binary operations
$\oplus,\ominus,\otimes$ on $\R$ such that for all $a,b\in\R$ there exist
$e_\oplus,e_\ominus,e_\otimes$ with $|e_\bullet|\le u$ and
\[
a\oplus b=(a+b)(1+e_\oplus),\qquad a\ominus b=(a-b)(1+e_\ominus),\qquad a\otimes b=ab(1+e_\otimes).
\]
\end{definition}

This is precisely the standard IEEE-754 model \emph{in the absence of overflow, underflow and
exceptional values}. The hypothesis ``the execution avoids overflow and exceptional values'' is
discharged, exactly and completely, by the assumption that the model applies to every operation
performed. No property of the bit-level format is used anywhere below, so every result applies
verbatim to any faithfully-rounded arithmetic. We do \emph{not} assume $u<1$: the semantic
results are unconditional in $u\ge0$, and hypotheses of the form $ku<1$ appear only where the
classical estimates require them.

\begin{definition}[Adversarial model]\label{def:adv}
For $u\ge0$ the \emph{uniform adversarial model} is $a\oplus b=(a+b)(1+u)$,
$a\ominus b=(a-b)(1+u)$, $a\otimes b=ab(1+u)$. It is a rounding model with unit roundoff $u$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}\label{def:gamma}
For $u\in\R$ and $k\in\N$, set $\gamma_k(u):=(1+u)^k-1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:gammabasic}
Let $u\ge0$. Then (i) $\gamma_k(u)\ge0$; (ii) $k\le l\implies\gamma_k(u)\le\gamma_l(u)$;
(iii) $u\le\gamma_k(u)$ for $k\ge1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
(i) $(1+u)^k\ge1$ since $1+u\ge1$. (ii) Monotonicity of $t\mapsto t^k$ in $k$ for base $\ge1$.
(iii) Take $k=1$ in (ii) and note $\gamma_1(u)=u$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Composition of relative perturbations]\label{lem:compose}
Let $u\ge0$ and suppose $|t_1-1|\le\gamma_a(u)$ and $|t_2-1|\le\gamma_b(u)$. Then
$|t_1t_2-1|\le\gamma_{a+b}(u)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Write $t_1t_2-1=(t_1-1)t_2+(t_2-1)$. Since $|t_2|\le1+\gamma_b(u)=(1+u)^b$,
\[
|t_1t_2-1|\le\gamma_a(u)(1+u)^b+\gamma_b(u)=\bigl((1+u)^a-1\bigr)(1+u)^b+(1+u)^b-1=(1+u)^{a+b}-1. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

This lemma is the engine of the theory: the exponent of $\gamma$ is an additive account of how
many roundings have touched a quantity.

\begin{theorem}[Classical form of the error constant]\label{thm:classical}
Let $u\ge0$ and $k\in\N$ with $ku<1$. Then $\gamma_k(u)\le \dfrac{ku}{1-ku}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $k$; for $k=0$ both sides vanish. Assume the bound for $k=n$ and let $(n+1)u<1$,
whence $nu<1$ and $1-nu>0$. From $\gamma_{n+1}(u)=\gamma_n(u)(1+u)+u$ and the inductive
hypothesis,
\[
\gamma_{n+1}(u)\le\frac{nu}{1-nu}(1+u)+u=\frac{(n+1)u}{1-nu},
\]
and since $0<1-(n+1)u\le1-nu$ with $(n+1)u\ge0$, the right side is at most
$(n+1)u/(1-(n+1)u)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Horner evaluation]\label{def:horner}
Represent a polynomial by its coefficient list $\bfa=(a_0,\dots,a_{n-1})$, meaning
$p(x)=\sum_{i<n}a_ix^i$. The exact evaluation is $\Ho((),x)=0$ and
$\Ho((a_0,\bfa'),x)=a_0+x\,\Ho(\bfa',x)$. The rounded evaluation in a rounding model is
$\Hf((),x)=0$ and
\[
\Hf((a_0,\bfa'),x)=a_0\oplus\bigl(x\otimes\Hf(\bfa',x)\bigr).
\]
\end{definition}

Thus an $n$-coefficient polynomial costs $n$ multiplications and $n$ additions: two roundings per
coefficient.

\begin{definition}[Magnitude functional]\label{def:ampl}
$\Ampl(\bfa,x):=\sum_{i<n}|a_i|\,|x|^i=\Ho\bigl((|a_0|,\dots,|a_{n-1}|),|x|\bigr)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:ampl}
$\Ampl\ge0$; $\Ampl((a_0,\bfa'),x)=|a_0|+|x|\Ampl(\bfa',x)$; and
$|x|\le B\implies\Ampl(\bfa,x)\le\Ampl(\bfa,B)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on the list, using the displayed recursion and nonnegativity of each summand.
\end{proof}

\section{The semantic layer I: backward error of one evaluation}

\begin{lemma}[Coefficientwise to evaluation]\label{lem:transfer}
If $|b_i-a_i|\le c|a_i|$ for all $i$, with $c\ge0$, then
$|\Ho(\bfb,x)-\Ho(\bfa,x)|\le c\,\Ampl(\bfa,x)$ for all $x$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on the list; the empty case is trivial. For $(b_0,\bfb')$ and $(a_0,\bfa')$,
$\Ho(\bfb,x)-\Ho(\bfa,x)=(b_0-a_0)+x(\Ho(\bfb',x)-\Ho(\bfa',x))$, which by the triangle
inequality and the inductive hypothesis is at most $c|a_0|+|x|c\Ampl(\bfa',x)=c\Ampl(\bfa,x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Rescaling degrades the exponent by two]\label{lem:rescale}
Let $u\ge0$. If $|b_i-a_i|\le\gamma_{2m}(u)|a_i|$ for all $i$ and $|t-1|\le\gamma_2(u)$, then
$|b_it-a_i|\le\gamma_{2m+2}(u)|a_i|$ for all $i$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $a_i\ne0$ write $b_i=a_is_i$ with $|s_i-1|\le\gamma_{2m}(u)$; then
$|s_it-1|\le\gamma_{2m+2}(u)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:compose}, and multiply by $|a_i|$. If $a_i=0$
then $b_i=0$ and the claim is trivial.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Backward-error semantics of rounded Horner evaluation]\label{thm:backward}
Fix a rounding model with unit roundoff $u\ge0$, a coefficient list $\bfa=(a_0,\dots,a_{n-1})$ and
a point $x\in\R$. Then there exists a real coefficient list $\bfb=(b_0,\dots,b_{n-1})$ with
\[
|b_i-a_i|\le\gamma_{2n}(u)\,|a_i|\qquad(0\le i<n)
\]
such that $\Hf(\bfa,x)=\Ho(\bfb,x)$. That is: the rounded evaluation of $p$ at $x$ is the
\textbf{exact} evaluation, at the same point $x$, of a polynomial whose coefficients are
relatively within $\gamma_{2n}(u)$ of those of $p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$. For $n=0$ both sides are $0$. For the step, write $\bfa=(a_0,\bfa')$ with
$\bfa'$ of length $m=n-1$, and let $\bfb'$ be supplied by the inductive hypothesis, so
$|b_i'-a_i'|\le\gamma_{2m}(u)|a_i'|$ and $\Hf(\bfa',x)=\Ho(\bfb',x)$. Let $v:=\Hf(\bfa',x)$. The
model supplies $e_2$ with $|e_2|\le u$ and $x\otimes v=xv(1+e_2)$, and $e_1$ with $|e_1|\le u$ and
$a_0\oplus(x\otimes v)=(a_0+xv(1+e_2))(1+e_1)$. Put $t:=(1+e_1)(1+e_2)$; by
Lemma~\ref{lem:compose} with $a=b=1$, $|t-1|\le\gamma_2(u)$. Then
\[
\Hf(\bfa,x)=a_0(1+e_1)+x\,v\,t=a_0(1+e_1)+x\,\Ho(\bfb',x)\,t=\Ho\bigl((a_0(1+e_1),\,t\bfb'),\,x\bigr),
\]
using that scaling every coefficient by $t$ scales the Horner value by $t$. Set
$\bfb:=(a_0(1+e_1),tb_0',\dots,tb_{m-1}')$. For the head,
$|a_0(1+e_1)-a_0|=|e_1||a_0|\le u|a_0|\le\gamma_{2m+2}(u)|a_0|$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:gammabasic}(iii);
for the tail, Lemma~\ref{lem:rescale} gives $|tb_i'-a_i'|\le\gamma_{2m+2}(u)|a_i'|$. Since
$2m+2=2n$, we are done.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Why the exponent is $2n$]
The exponent is forced by the induction: each Horner level performs two roundings and \emph{both}
multiply every previously perturbed coefficient by a common factor. A graded refinement in which
$a_i$ carries only $\gamma_{2i+2}$ is true and follows from the same induction, but the uniform
bound is what the dynamical layer consumes and what composes cleanly under iteration.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Strictness]
Theorem~\ref{thm:backward} is strictly stronger than any forward bound: it identifies the exact
problem solved and localizes the perturbation to the data, allowing the conditioning of the
evaluation to be assessed separately. Near an ill-conditioned root the forward error can be
$100\%$ while the backward error remains at the sixteenth digit.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Local defect certificate]\label{thm:defect}
For any rounding model with unit roundoff $u\ge0$, any $\bfa$ of length $n$ and any $x$,
\[
\bigl|\Hf(\bfa,x)-\Ho(\bfa,x)\bigr|\le\gamma_{2n}(u)\,\Ampl(\bfa,x)
=\gamma_{2n}(u)\sum_{i<n}|a_i||x|^i.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:backward} and Lemma~\ref{lem:transfer}.
\end{proof}

This is a \emph{compositional expression in the unit roundoff and the intermediate magnitudes}: a
precision factor depending only on the arithmetic and the program length, times a magnitude factor
depending only on the data.

\begin{theorem}[Exact defect in the adversarial model]\label{thm:advdefect}
In the uniform adversarial model with parameter $u\ge0$, evaluating the constant polynomial
$\bfa=(a)$ at any $x$ gives $\bigl|\Hf((a),x)-\Ho((a),x)\bigr|=u\,\Ampl((a),x)=u|a|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\Hf((a),x)=a\oplus(x\otimes0)=a(1+u)$, $\Ho((a),x)=a$, $\Ampl((a),x)=|a|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The first-order term cannot be removed]\label{thm:sharpdefect}
For $0\le u\le1$ and any $a,x$, in the uniform adversarial model,
\[
\tfrac13\,\gamma_2(u)\,\Ampl((a),x)\ \le\ \bigl|\Hf((a),x)-\Ho((a),x)\bigr|.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:advdefect} the right side is $u|a|$, while $\gamma_2(u)=2u+u^2\le3u$ for
$u\le1$.
\end{proof}

Thus Theorem~\ref{thm:defect} is correct to within a factor $3$ in the worst case for $n=1$, and
no bound of order $u^2$ can hold: the theory is first-order tight.

\section{The semantic layer II: executions are certified pseudo-orbits}

\begin{definition}\label{def:po}
A sequence $(x_k)_{k\ge0}$ is a \emph{$\delta$-pseudo-orbit of $f$ for $N$ steps} if
$|x_{k+1}-f(x_k)|\le\delta$ for all $k<N$. The \emph{true orbit} of $f$ through $y_0$ is
$f^k(y_0)$. Given a rounding model and a coefficient list $\bfa$, the \emph{floating-point orbit}
through $x_0$ is $X_0:=x_0$, $X_{k+1}:=\Hf(\bfa,X_k)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Semantic translation]\label{thm:translation}
Let $\bfa$ have length $n$, let $(X_k)$ be the floating-point orbit through $x_0$ in a rounding
model with unit roundoff $u\ge0$, and suppose $|X_k|\le B$ for all $k\le N$. Then $(X_k)$ is a
$\delta$-pseudo-orbit of the exact map $p(z)=\Ho(\bfa,z)$ for $N$ steps, with
\[
\delta=\gamma_{2n}(u)\,\Ampl(\bfa,B)=\gamma_{2n}(u)\sum_{i<n}|a_i|B^i.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $k<N$, $X_{k+1}=\Hf(\bfa,X_k)$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:defect} gives
$|X_{k+1}-p(X_k)|\le\gamma_{2n}(u)\Ampl(\bfa,X_k)$, and Lemma~\ref{lem:ampl} with $|X_k|\le B$
gives $\Ampl(\bfa,X_k)\le\Ampl(\bfa,B)$. Multiply by $\gamma_{2n}(u)\ge0$.
\end{proof}

Two features deserve emphasis. First, \emph{unconditionality in the dynamics}: no hypothesis on
$p$ appears. Second, the hypothesis that \emph{is} used, ``$|X_k|\le B$ for $k\le N$'', is not an
a-priori assumption but a runtime check on the program's own output, and it is exactly the
certificate that no overflow or exceptional value occurred. It is non-vacuous: for the logistic
map at $r=4$, the exact orbit of any $x_0\in[0,1]$ remains in $[0,1]$.

\begin{theorem}[Backward-error semantics of the whole execution]\label{thm:nonauto}
In the setting of Theorem~\ref{thm:translation}, for each $k$ there is a coefficient list
$\bfb^{(k)}$ with $|b_i^{(k)}-a_i|\le\gamma_{2n}(u)|a_i|$ for all $i$, such that
$X_{k+1}=\Ho(\bfb^{(k)},X_k)$ \textbf{exactly}. Hence the execution is the exact orbit of a
nonautonomous polynomial system whose coefficient sequence lies in a relative
$\gamma_{2n}(u)$-tube around $\bfa$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:backward} at the point $X_k$ for each $k$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:nonauto} is the precise form of the informal conjecture that every finite
execution avoiding overflow and exceptional values can be translated into an exact real
pseudo-orbit whose local defect is bounded by a compositional expression in the unit roundoff and
the intermediate magnitudes; Theorem~\ref{thm:translation} is its quantitative shadow.

\section{The dynamical layer I: forward shadowing and its sharpness}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-time shadowing]\label{thm:finite}
Let $f:\R\to\R$, $L\ge0$, and $S\subseteq\R$ with $|f(a)-f(b)|\le L|a-b|$ for all $a,b\in S$. Let
$(x_k)$ be a $\delta$-pseudo-orbit of $f$ for $N$ steps with $x_k\in S$ and $f^k(x_0)\in S$ for
$k\le N$. Then for all $n\le N$,
\[
\bigl|x_n-f^{\,n}(x_0)\bigr|\le\delta\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}L^k
=\begin{cases}\delta\dfrac{L^n-1}{L-1},&L\ne1,\\[2mm] n\delta,&L=1.\end{cases}
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$; the case $n=0$ is trivial. With $y_k:=f^k(x_0)$,
$x_{n+1}-y_{n+1}=(x_{n+1}-f(x_n))+(f(x_n)-f(y_n))$, so
$|x_{n+1}-y_{n+1}|\le\delta+L|x_n-y_n|\le\delta+L\delta\sum_{k<n}L^k=\delta\sum_{k<n+1}L^k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Contractions]\label{cor:contract}
If moreover $0\le L<1$ and $\delta\ge0$, then $|x_n-f^n(x_0)|\le\delta/(1-L)$ for all $n\le N$,
uniformly in $n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The forward bound is attained]\label{thm:sharpshadow}
For every $L\ge0$ and $\delta\ge0$ there exist a map $f:\R\to\R$ with $|f(a)-f(b)|=L|a-b|$ for
\emph{all} $a,b$, and a sequence $(x_k)$ with $|x_{k+1}-f(x_k)|=\delta$ for \emph{all} $k$, such
that $\bigl|x_n-f^{\,n}(x_0)\bigr|=\delta\sum_{k<n}L^k$ for all $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $f(z)=Lz$, $x_0=0$, $x_{k+1}=Lx_k+\delta$. Then $|f(a)-f(b)|=L|a-b|$ and
$|x_{k+1}-f(x_k)|=\delta$ identically; the true orbit through $0$ is identically $0$, while
induction gives $x_n=\delta\sum_{k<n}L^k\ge0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The exponential is dynamical, not arithmetical]\label{cor:notimprovable}
For $L>1$, $\delta>0$ and every $n$ there is an exactly $L$-Lipschitz map and a sequence whose
per-step defect is exactly $\delta$ with $|x_n-f^n(x_0)|=\delta(L^n-1)/(L-1)$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Instantiation: the logistic map in binary64}

Let $f(z):=4z(1-z)$ with coefficient list $\bfa=(0,4,-4)$, so $\Ho(\bfa,z)=f(z)$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:invariant}
$f([0,1])\subseteq[0,1]$; consequently $f^n(y_0)\in[0,1]$ for all $n$ whenever $y_0\in[0,1]$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For $z\in[0,1]$, $4z(1-z)\ge0$, and $1-4z(1-z)=(2z-1)^2\ge0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:lip4}
$|f(a)-f(b)|\le4|a-b|$ for $a,b\in[0,1]$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$f(a)-f(b)=(a-b)\cdot4(1-a-b)$ and $|4(1-a-b)|\le4$ for $a,b\in[0,1]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Defect bound in binary64]\label{lem:b64}
If $0\le u\le2^{-53}$ then $\gamma_6(u)\,\Ampl(\bfa,1)\le2^{-46}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\Ampl((0,4,-4),1)=8$. Since $6u\le6\cdot2^{-53}<\tfrac12$, Theorem~\ref{thm:classical} gives
$\gamma_6(u)\le6u/(1-6u)\le12u\le12\cdot2^{-53}$. Hence
$\gamma_6(u)\cdot8\le96\cdot2^{-53}<128\cdot2^{-53}=2^{-46}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Certified shadowing of a binary64 logistic execution]\label{thm:logistic}
Let $(X_k)$ be the floating-point orbit of $f(z)=4z(1-z)$ evaluated by Horner's rule in any
rounding model with $u\le2^{-53}$, started at $x_0\in[0,1]$, and suppose the execution is
\emph{observed} to satisfy $X_k\in[0,1]$ for all $k\le N$. Then for all $n\le N$,
\[
\bigl|X_n-f^{\,n}(x_0)\bigr|\le2^{-46}\,\frac{4^n-1}{3}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:translation} with $B=1$, $(X_k)$ is a $\delta$-pseudo-orbit with
$\delta=\gamma_6(u)\Ampl(\bfa,1)\le2^{-46}$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:b64}). By Lemma~\ref{lem:invariant}
the true orbit stays in $[0,1]$, and by hypothesis so does the execution, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:finite} applies on $S=[0,1]$ with $L=4$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:lip4}), giving
$\delta\sum_{k<n}4^k=\delta(4^n-1)/3$.
\end{proof}

The bound is fully attributable: $2^{-46}$ comes only from the arithmetic, $(4^n-1)/3$ only from
the dynamics. Setting the bound to $1$ (the diameter of the state space) gives $n\approx23$: the
certified horizon of a double-precision logistic simulation under forward tracking, in agreement
with the practitioners' heuristic of losing $\log_{10}4\approx0.6$ decimal digits per step.

\section{The dynamical layer II: uniform-in-time shadowing for expanding maps}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform-in-time backward shadowing]\label{thm:uniform}
Let $f:\R\to\R$, $\lambda>1$, $\delta\ge0$. Suppose given inverse branches $g_n:\R\to\R$
($n\in\N$) with
\[
f(g_n(z))=z,\qquad |g_n(z)-g_n(w)|\le\frac{|z-w|}{\lambda}\quad\text{for all }z,w,
\]
and let $(x_k)$ be a $\delta$-pseudo-orbit of $f$ for $N$ steps compatible with the branches in the
sense $g_n(f(x_n))=x_n$ for all $n$. Then there exists a genuine orbit $(y_k)$ of $f$, i.e.\
$f(y_k)=y_{k+1}$ for $k<N$, with
\[
|y_n-x_n|\le\frac{\delta}{\lambda-1}\qquad\text{for all }n\le N,
\]
a bound \textbf{independent of $N$}.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $N$, generalizing over the pseudo-orbit and the branch family. For $N=0$ take
$y_k\equiv x_0$. For the step, apply the inductive hypothesis to the shifted data $x_k':=x_{k+1}$,
$g_n':=g_{n+1}$, obtaining an orbit $(y_k')$ with $|y_n'-x_{n+1}|\le\delta/(\lambda-1)$ for
$n\le N$. Define $y_0:=g_0(y_0')$ and $y_{k+1}:=y_k'$. Then $f(y_0)=y_0'=y_1$ and the orbit
relation for $k\ge1$ is inherited. For $n=0$, using $g_0(f(x_0))=x_0$ and contraction,
\[
|y_0-x_0|=|g_0(y_0')-g_0(f(x_0))|\le\frac{|y_0'-f(x_0)|}{\lambda}
\le\frac{|y_0'-x_1|+|x_1-f(x_0)|}{\lambda}\le\frac{\frac{\delta}{\lambda-1}+\delta}{\lambda}
=\frac{\delta}{\lambda-1},
\]
the last equality because $\delta/(\lambda-1)$ is the fixed point of $E\mapsto(\delta+E)/\lambda$.
\end{proof}

The mechanism is transparent: the induction runs backwards along the time horizon, and each
backward step is a $1/\lambda$-contraction, so accumulated error converges to a fixed point rather
than diverging geometrically. The price is that $y_0\ne x_0$.

\subsection{A concrete expanding polynomial}

Let $p(z):=z^3+2z$ with coefficient list $\bfc=(0,2,0,1)$, so $\Ho(\bfc,z)=p(z)$ and
$\Ampl(\bfc,B)=2B+B^3$ for $B\ge0$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:cubic}
(i) $|p(a)-p(b)|\ge2|a-b|$ for all $a,b$; (ii) $p$ is a bijection of $\R$; (iii) $p^{-1}$
satisfies $|p^{-1}(z)-p^{-1}(w)|\le|z-w|/2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
(i) $p(a)-p(b)=(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2+2)$ and $a^2+ab+b^2=\tfrac12(a^2+b^2+(a+b)^2)\ge0$, so the second
factor is $\ge2$. (ii) Injectivity from (i); surjectivity from continuity and
$p(z)\to\pm\infty$ by the intermediate value theorem. (iii) Substitute $a=p^{-1}(z)$,
$b=p^{-1}(w)$ in (i).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform certified shadowing of an expanding-cubic execution]\label{thm:cubic}
Let $(X_k)$ be the floating-point orbit of $p(z)=z^3+2z$ by Horner's rule in a rounding model with
unit roundoff $u\ge0$, started at any $x_0$, and suppose $|X_k|\le B$ for all $k\le N$. Then there
exists a \textbf{genuine} orbit $(y_k)$ of $p$ with
\[
|y_n-X_n|\le\gamma_8(u)\bigl(2B+B^3\bigr)\qquad\text{for all }n\le N,
\]
a bound independent of $N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\bfc$ has length $4$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:translation} makes $(X_k)$ a $\delta$-pseudo-orbit of
$p$ with $\delta=\gamma_8(u)(2B+B^3)$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:uniform} with $g_n:=p^{-1}$ and
$\lambda=2$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:cubic}); the conclusion is $|y_n-X_n|\le\delta/(2-1)=\delta$.
\end{proof}

For $B=1$ and $u=2^{-53}$: $\gamma_8(u)\le16\cdot2^{-53}$ and $2B+B^3=3$, so the certified error is
below $6\times10^{-15}$ for \emph{every} $n$. The exponential catastrophe of
Theorem~\ref{thm:logistic} is thus attributable entirely to the rigidity of forward tracking.

\begin{remark}[Expansivity is essential]
Theorem~\ref{thm:uniform} does not silently subsume Theorem~\ref{thm:logistic}: $f(z)=4z(1-z)$ has
a critical point at $z=\tfrac12$ where $f'=0$, so it admits no globally $1/\lambda$-Lipschitz
inverse branch on $[0,1]$; it is two-to-one. Extending the uniform bound to non-invertible maps
requires local inverse branches plus a combinatorial choice of itinerary.
\end{remark}

\section{The dynamical layer III: a-posteriori nonautonomous certificates}

\begin{definition}\label{def:errbound}
Given $\delta\ge0$ and $L:\N\to[0,\infty)$, define the \emph{a-posteriori error recursion}
$E_0:=0$, $E_{n+1}:=\delta+L_nE_n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:errbound}
$E_n\ge0$; $E$ is monotone in $\delta$; and if $L_n\equiv L$ then $E_n=\delta\sum_{k<n}L^k$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Each by induction, the last using $\sum_{k<n+1}L^k=L\sum_{k<n}L^k+1$.
\end{proof}

The last clause guarantees that the nonautonomous bound is never worse than the autonomous one of
Theorem~\ref{thm:finite}: it refines it.

\begin{theorem}[Nonautonomous a-posteriori shadowing]\label{thm:variable}
Let $f:\R\to\R$, $S\subseteq\R$, $\delta\ge0$, $L:\N\to[0,\infty)$. Let $(x_k)$ be a
$\delta$-pseudo-orbit of $f$ for $N$ steps, suppose $f^k(x_0)\in S$ for $k\le N$, and suppose the
one-sided local Lipschitz estimate
\[
|f(x_n)-f(b)|\le L_n|x_n-b|\qquad\text{for all }n<N,\ b\in S.
\]
Then $|x_n-f^{\,n}(x_0)|\le E_n$ for all $n\le N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$, as in Theorem~\ref{thm:finite} but using $L_n$ at step $n$:
$|x_{n+1}-y_{n+1}|\le\delta+L_n|x_n-y_n|\le\delta+L_nE_n=E_{n+1}$.
\end{proof}

The Lipschitz estimate is required only \emph{at the observed point} $x_n$, against all comparison
points in $S$; this is what makes it a-posteriori-checkable.

\begin{lemma}[Local expansion of the logistic map]\label{lem:loclip}
For $a\in[0,1]$ and all $b\in[0,1]$, with $f(z)=4z(1-z)$,
\[
|f(a)-f(b)|\le4\max(a,1-a)\,|a-b|.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$f(a)-f(b)=(a-b)\cdot4(1-a-b)$; for $b\in[0,1]$, $4(1-a-b)\le4(1-a)\le4\max(a,1-a)$ and
$4(1-a-b)\ge-4a\ge-4\max(a,1-a)$.
\end{proof}

The factor $4\max(a,1-a)$ lies in $[2,4]$, equals $4$ only at the endpoints and $2$ at
$a=\tfrac12$.

\begin{theorem}[A-posteriori certificate for a binary64 logistic execution]\label{thm:apost}
Let $(X_k)$ be the floating-point orbit of $4z(1-z)$ by Horner's rule with $u\le2^{-53}$, from
$x_0\in[0,1]$, observed to satisfy $X_k\in[0,1]$ for $k\le N$. Then for all $n\le N$,
\[
\bigl|X_n-f^{\,n}(x_0)\bigr|\le E_n,\qquad E_0=0,\quad
E_{n+1}=2^{-46}+4\max\bigl(X_n,1-X_n\bigr)E_n.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:translation} and Lemma~\ref{lem:b64} give the pseudo-orbit property with
$\delta\le2^{-46}$; Lemma~\ref{lem:invariant} places the true orbit in $S=[0,1]$;
Lemma~\ref{lem:loclip} supplies the local factors; Theorem~\ref{thm:variable} concludes, and
monotonicity of $E$ in $\delta$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:errbound}) allows replacing the exact $\delta$ by
$2^{-46}$.
\end{proof}

Every quantity on the right is computed from the execution itself: one runs the iteration and the
recursion $E$ side by side and stops trusting the output when $E_n$ exceeds a tolerance.

\section{Structural backward error: a property of the program}

\begin{definition}\label{def:step}
The \emph{product-form} logistic step is the three-operation program
$\mathrm{Step}(r,x):=r\otimes\bigl(x\otimes(1\ominus x)\bigr)$, approximating
$\Phi_r(x):=r\,x(1-x)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Structural backward error]\label{thm:structural}
For any rounding model with unit roundoff $u\ge0$ and any $r,x$, there is $r'\in\R$ with
\[
|r'-r|\le\gamma_3(u)|r|\qquad\text{and}\qquad\mathrm{Step}(r,x)=\Phi_{r'}(x)\ \textbf{exactly}.
\]
The floating-point step is an exact logistic step of the same family, at a detuned parameter.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The model supplies $e_1,e_2,e_3$ with $|e_i|\le u$ and
$1\ominus x=(1-x)(1+e_1)$, $x\otimes(1\ominus x)=x(1-x)(1+e_1)(1+e_2)$, and
$\mathrm{Step}(r,x)=r\,x(1-x)(1+e_1)(1+e_2)(1+e_3)$. Put
$r':=r(1+e_1)(1+e_2)(1+e_3)$; then $\mathrm{Step}(r,x)=\Phi_{r'}(x)$ and
$|r'-r|=|(1+e_1)(1+e_2)(1+e_3)-1||r|\le\gamma_3(u)|r|$ by two applications of
Lemma~\ref{lem:compose}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$|\mathrm{Step}(r,x)-\Phi_r(x)|\le\gamma_3(u)\,|r|\,|x|\,|1-x|$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[A floating-point logistic run is an exact nonautonomous logistic family]
\label{thm:family}
Let $Y_0:=x_0$ and $Y_{k+1}:=\mathrm{Step}(r,Y_k)$. Then there is a sequence $(r_k)$ with
$|r_k-r|\le\gamma_3(u)|r|$ for all $k$ and $Y_{k+1}=\Phi_{r_k}(Y_k)$ for all $k$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:structural} at each $Y_k$ and collect the parameters.
\end{proof}

Consistency with the coefficient picture is immediate: $\Phi_r(z)=\Ho((0,r,-r),z)$, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:family} strengthens Theorem~\ref{thm:nonauto} for this particular program: the
perturbed coefficient list can be taken of the special form $(0,r',-r')$, i.e.\ the perturbation
respects the parametric family, whereas Theorem~\ref{thm:nonauto} alone would allow the two nonzero
coefficients to be perturbed independently.

\begin{remark}[Why the expression matters]
The proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:structural} uses essentially that the symbol $r$ occurs \emph{once}
in the expression, so all three roundings can be collected onto it. The algebraically equivalent
expanded program $(r\otimes x)\ominus(r\otimes(x\otimes x))$ contains two occurrences of $r$, which
receive independent distortions, and the result is generally not $\Phi_{r'}$ for any single $r'$.
Structural backward error is therefore a \emph{syntactic} invariant of the evaluation graph ---
parameter multiplicity --- rather than a property of the function computed.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Boundary of the theory: detuning can destroy invariance]\label{thm:boundary}
If $r>4$ then $\Phi_r(\tfrac12)=r/4\notin[0,1]$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\Phi_r(1/2)=r\cdot\tfrac12\cdot\tfrac12=r/4>1$.
\end{proof}

Small as it is, the parameter perturbation of Theorem~\ref{thm:structural} can push $r$ across the
threshold $4$, after which $[0,1]$ is no longer invariant and the orbit escapes. Consequently no
a-priori argument can dispense with the observed hypothesis ``the execution remained in $[0,1]$''
in Theorems~\ref{thm:logistic} and \ref{thm:apost}: it is load-bearing, and it is exactly the
runtime check the semantics layer requires.

\section{Algorithms}

Three algorithms follow directly. All add $O(1)$ per iteration, so certification is essentially
free.

\paragraph{Algorithm A --- Certified defect.} Given $\bfa$ of length $n$, an observed magnitude
bound $B$ and a unit roundoff $u$, return $\delta=\gamma_{2n}(u)\sum_i|a_i|B^i$. Cost $O(n)$;
correctness by Theorem~\ref{thm:translation}.

\paragraph{Algorithm B --- Instrumented iteration with a-posteriori bound.} Run
$X_{k+1}=\Hf(\bfa,X_k)$; at each step check $|X_k|\le B$ (abort if violated: the semantic
hypothesis has failed); update $E_{k+1}=\delta+L_kE_k$ with $L_k$ the observed local expansion
factor at $X_k$; report the certified horizon $\max\{n:E_n\le\texttt{tol}\}$. Cost $O(n)$ per
step; correctness by Theorem~\ref{thm:variable}.

\paragraph{Algorithm C --- Backward coefficient reconstruction.} Replay the Horner recursion
recording the two per-level rounding factors and emit the perturbed coefficient list $\bfb$ of
Theorem~\ref{thm:backward} explicitly, together with the verification $\Hf(\bfa,x)=\Ho(\bfb,x)$ to
the last bit. Cost $O(n)$; this turns the semantics theorem into a witness that hands the user the
exact polynomial the machine solved.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

\subsection{What the separation buys}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
\item \emph{Layer (S) is improvable by better arithmetic, layer (D) is not.} Doubling the
precision divides $\delta$ by $2^{53}$ and extends the certified horizon of
Theorem~\ref{thm:logistic} by $\log_42^{53}\approx26$ steps --- linearly in the number of bits,
exactly as folklore predicts, but now as a theorem.
\item \emph{Layer (D) is improvable by asking a better question, layer (S) is not.}
Theorem~\ref{thm:cubic} achieves a bound uniform in $n$ with identical arithmetic, purely by
allowing the shadowing orbit to start elsewhere.
\item \emph{The interface is lossless.} Theorem~\ref{thm:defect} follows from
Theorem~\ref{thm:backward} in two lines; the converse is impossible, since the forward bound does
not determine which perturbed problem was solved.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Limitations}

The theory covers univariate polynomial iterations evaluated by Horner's rule in a relative-error
rounding model. Extensions to multivariate systems, to rational and transcendental primitives, to
division, and to underflow (where the relative model must be replaced by a mixed
relative/absolute one) are natural and open. Theorem~\ref{thm:uniform} requires global inverse
branches; extending it to piecewise-expanding maps with critical points requires tracking
itineraries.

\subsection{Future directions}

\paragraph{1. Structural backward error is a property of the evaluation scheme, not the
polynomial.} \emph{Conjecture.} For a parameterised polynomial family $p_\theta$, a straight-line
evaluation program admits family-structural backward error (every rounded execution equals the
exact $p_{\theta'}$ for some $\theta'$ with $|\theta'-\theta|\le\gamma_k(u)|\theta|$) \emph{iff}
the program's expression graph factors through a single occurrence of each parameter. The key
insight is that structural backward error is a syntactic invariant of the graph (parameter
multiplicity), not a numerical property. Theorem~\ref{thm:structural} proves the positive
direction for the single-occurrence product form $r\otimes(x\otimes(1\ominus x))$ and identifies
the expanded form $rx-rx^2$, where $r$ occurs twice, as the natural candidate counterexample; the
general statement is a finite combinatorial claim about expression graphs.

\paragraph{2. Nonautonomous shadowing with observed local expansion beats the global constant.}
\emph{Conjecture.} For the logistic map at $r=4$ the a-posteriori recursion
$E_{n+1}=\delta+|4-8X_n|E_n$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:variable} grows like $e^{n\ln2}$, not $4^n$, for
Lebesgue-almost every initial condition, so the certified shadowing horizon doubles. The key
insight is that the Birkhoff average of $\ln|f'|$ against the natural invariant density
$1/(\pi\sqrt{z(1-z)})$ is $\ln2$, not $\ln4$.

\paragraph{3. Uniform shadowing beyond invertibility.} Extend Theorem~\ref{thm:uniform} to maps
with critical points by choosing inverse branches along an itinerary, and determine whether a
double-precision logistic execution is shadowed uniformly in time by \emph{some} exact orbit.

\paragraph{4. Mixed relative/absolute models.} Replace Definition~\ref{def:model} by
$\mathrm{fl}(a\circ b)=(a\circ b)(1+e)+\eta$ with $|e|\le u$, $|\eta|\le\eta_{\min}$ to cover
gradual underflow, and determine how Theorem~\ref{thm:defect} degrades near the bottom of the
exponent range.

\paragraph{5. Interval and stochastic refinements.} A probabilistic version --- treating the $e_i$
as independent bounded random variables --- should replace $\gamma_{2n}(u)$ by $O(\sqrt{n}\,u)$
with high probability, giving a horizon extension of $\tfrac12\log_Ln$ steps.

\section{Conclusion}

The claim that floating-point simulation of chaos is meaningless conflates a semantic question
with a dynamical one. Separated, both become tractable and both become sharp. A finite
floating-point execution of a polynomial iteration, free of overflow and exceptional values, is
not an approximation to anything: it is the exact orbit of a nonautonomous polynomial system whose
coefficients lie within relative distance $\gamma_{2n}(u)$ of the nominal ones, hence an exact
pseudo-orbit with the compositional defect $\gamma_{2n}(u)\sum_i|a_i|B^i$. That certificate is
unconditional in the dynamics and checkable at runtime. Handing it to a dynamical theorem then
yields explicit, attributable error bounds: exponential and provably unimprovable under forward
tracking; uniform in time under expansivity; and, in the a-posteriori nonautonomous form,
computable by the running program about itself. The exponential loss of accuracy in chaotic
simulation is real, but it is a fact about dynamics, not about arithmetic --- and knowing exactly
which problem the machine solved is more useful than knowing how far its answer drifted.

